rained  in a self-supervised way where there is  no human labeling but the data itself  is used to guide the training  so given a sentence you can mask out  some words and  make the neural network predict the  missing words from their context  a kind of fill in the blanks exercise  and  learning to do these apparently simple  tasks that  for example my six years old son  routinely does it at school  it actually requires a deeper  understanding of the structure of the  language  and neural networks appear to be capable  of doing it provided  a large corpus of examples it also  appears that  pre-training a neural network in such a  way produces embeddings that  are somehow almost universal they can be  used for many downstream tasks  so for example a simple classifier  applied to the world embeddings  can detect if the input comes from the  spam email or the sentiment of the text  is happy said or exciting or  any other task that you want to do with  your language  still machine translation relied on  known translations or corresponding  words across languages  and that is to say until recently in  2018 a group from facebook ai research  showed that  it is possible to do completely  unsupervised translation  and for this purpose the embedding four  words in the language can be considered  as  some kind of high dimensional point  cloud that is shown here  just in two dimensions but imagine the  same thing in 200 dimensions  and if we train embeddings of two  languages say english and italian as  shown here  completely independently by this idea of  filling  the blanks we get two such point clouds  and it appears that they have similar  structure and can be geometrically  aligned basically by  this multi-dimensional rotation maybe a  little bit of non-rigid  alignment as well in this high  dimensional space producing  essentially automatic correspondence  between words or  what we call a translation and it seems  to be  working in languages that are as  dissimilar as english and chinese  so how large a data set is needed  to train modern language models let me  give you an idea of the order of  magnitude  so a model from 2018 called birth use  the data set collected from the english  wikipedia  and it contained roughly 2.5 billion  words  uh the generative pre-trained  transformer or gpk2 model developed by  openai uh used a bigger data set of  about 10 billion  words or tokens and in your version of  the model gpt3 that that  is now creating a lot of hype in the  machine learning community  released just a few months ago it used  really a colossal data set of half a  trillion words  and as a side note i should say that  this gpt3 model  is probably the largest and more complex  neural network that has ever been  trained  it has 175 billion parameters and the  estimate of the cost of training such a  model i  read some numbers that are uh totally  mind-blowing  uh uh north of five million dollars so  think of  the amount of energy that that is spent  in in trading such such neural network  so let me go back to strongwell because  i think we took a detour  in uh natural language processing that  that hopefully tomorrow we'll hear  many more details about it so project  seti  is an initiative as david mentioned that  tries to harness  their progress in deep learning in order  to gain new understanding of  the communication of sperm whales and  before attempting to apply  modern machine learning technology to  whale by acoustic data as we know it is  very data hungry we need to ask a  crucial question are we anywhere close  to these  uh gigantic data set scales  so in order to estimate how much data  well communication produces  we can do a simple back of the envelope  calculation  and uh as uh shane mentioned and he's  talking the area of  around 20 square kilometers off the  shore of dominica that  he and his team have been studying for  the past 15 years  between 50 and 400 whales are observed  it obviously depends on the season  and other situations these whales  vocalize nearly continuously with again  estimated  about 70 percent of their vocalizations  used for echolocation  and the remaining 25 of the clicks  are used for communication what we call  the codeclicks  and in the caribbean across different  locations  the code structures fry but uh the  typical coder  in the caribbean has five clicks and  lasts approximately four seconds  which produces a rate of information of  about  roughly slightly more than one symbol  per second  and overall this uh gets us to the range  between uh 400 million to four billion  clicks per year  so this is obviously a rough estimate  probably somewhere somewhat optimistic  upper bound  but it gives us the ballpark of the  amount of data that we can potentially  collect  and use for a machine learning pipeline  and you can see that it's comparable in  size to what is used in human language  analysis  just to give you a comparison uh the  dominica spread well project probably  one of the largest bioacoustic data sets  that shane and his team have been  collecting in the past  15 years and that we've been using so  far  has less than 100 000 uh annotated  clicks  so you can do the calculation yourself  it's orders of taking too  less so we're talking about a  qualitatively larger data set  that we believe to be really a game  changer in the field  so let me say a few words about what  kind of data we are trying to collect  first of all just a massive collection  of vocalizations that will allow us to  train  unsupervised world embedding kind of  models so same way  as uh in human nlp research  uh basically massive amounts of text  collected from anywhere like english  wikipedia  without really any innovation issues uh  just to predict uh the structure of the  language  and as participating so far  our another approach was to consider  codas as the basic communication units  and in this way we have prototypical  patterns of sequences of clicks  with uh certain intra click intervals or  icis which are roughly equivalent to  human words or at least this is  a convenient analogy and this might be  an overly simplified assumption since  codas are produced by  clustering and we see a great deal of  variability between colors of the same  type  and we don't know whether this is due to  variations in neutrons similar to the  different  manner to different people would  pronounce the same word  same way as uh when i speak and somebody  else thinks we  have slightly different uh pronunciation  or if there is a fundamental variation  that conveys meaning  which we are not taking into account so  one thing for sure is  to try to revisit from the from the  foundations  the entire concept of colors and basic  units of  sperm whale speech  furthermore the assumption of clicks  being broadband pulses and  only intervals between them carrying  information might be too simplistic and  shane mentioned it and patricia  mentioned it as well  we see evidence that clicks differ in  their spectral content and amplitude  and there is some evidence but we still  lack a clear understanding whether  uh which of these variations result from  recording artifacts  which come from the sound or production  mechanisms such as  maybe reverberations inside the head of  the whale that produces  these different interpol's intervals and  which of them are intentional and uh  whether these  spectral cues are used by the whales and  their communication  so same way as we can recognize they  check each other by the spectral  characteristics of our voices  we see that whales might be using these  cues but  i think it's not certain yet i'm not  even sure that whales  you have the anatomical capability  to intentionally control some of these  parameters of their voice  so i think there are many questions that  still need to be answered at  all levels of fields of expertise  secondly we are interested in collecting  whale conversations where we have  a dialogue between two whales or maybe  conversations  with more whales and this will allow  training more complex complex machine  learning models  that are not just trying to predict the  next intra click interval  or coda but also the response of while  one whale to another and if we're able  to reach certain acres here in this task  we will attempt to play back predicted  responses  and think of it as a kind of interactive  whale chatbot  and several attempts that that we are  aware of in the literature  of people trying to play back playback  sounds  to the whales but to the best of my  knowledge it has never been really an  interactive uh machine learning system  that tries also to  respond in real time to what uh what it  hears from from the web  and finally the overall structure of the  data set we are trying to collect is  uh a network of interactions uh between  wales  and because of my job at twitter i like  this social network metaphor  we are trying to build the whale twitter  and uh the nodes of the social graph are  whales for which we know the identities  demographic data their location and some  behavioral metadata over time  and the agents are instances of  communication or interactions between  different whales or maybe groups of  whales so we might even talk about  more complex structures than just edges  so when i said in the beginning that the  success of deep running can be  attributed uh primarily to the  availability of  large scale data sets uh some people  even call this the  the data the new oil i think it was  andrew and  that that produced this this analogy  uh in the sense that that modern machine  learning techniques are very data hungry  and require massive amounts of  currently created data that sometimes is  very expensive to produce so  once you have the data that uh it solves  at least half of the problem  so the data set that we are trying to  build here both in size  and the level of details and  complexities completely unprinted  unprecedented in marine biology  and collecting this data set is really a  gigantic effort  that requires a synergy of different  expertise and this brings me to the  third pillar of project city which is  key to its future success  and this is the team and  uh i think in this regard we have the  most amazing team ever possible and i'm  really truly honored to be part of it  so david uh who is also the uh  one of the organizers of this workshop  hence the entire collaboration  and uh as david and jaffe mentioned we  met  during the wonderful year at the  broadcave institute for advanced study  at harvard  where we all were fellows  and uh shafi organized  a series of machine learning seminars  where we discussed different papers and  invited  different speakers and david showed us  the whale  sounds and also organized an evening  where  we all uh watched the star trek movie i  don't know if you're  fans of of this series about there is uh  one movie about where there are whales  or there are not sperm whales i think  humpback whales  that travel in in the future  but bottom line basically we got i got  interested in this  in this project and we teamed up with  shane  has been working on sperm whales and is  one of  the biggest authorities in this domain  and uh  we wrote this proof of concept paper  with  either being a berman and and uh robot  which really tried to use uh  machine learning techniques of the the  modern types such as  deep neural networks uh to try some of  the the problems  that were before solved uh using uh  older techniques and uh the the  advantage of using machine learning  techniques  uh that they allow to build models from  the data rather than  handcrafting them so uh i mentioned  shane who  heads the the biology work we also have  as our advisor  roger payne a pioneer of way of research  that  discovered the whale songs in the 1970s  on the engineering and sensor design  part which is extremely important  with a very an underwater acoustics  expert  and a professor at the university of  haifa and  on the all the operations uh the the ceo  of the collaboration is dan chernov has  experience in building  marine stations in different places in  the world  so robotics plays an important part as  well and  here we have rob wood a pioneer of soft  robots for real life exploration and  daniela rose  who is also part of the machine learning  team who has developed some of the first  uh underwater autonomous robots and  on the side of data analysis and machine  learning we have antonio toralba jacob  andreas  and patricia sharma whom we met in the  previous talk  from mit and shafik goldwasser and  myself  on the linguistic side we have kevin  ryan from harvard  and we also have giovanni petty from  from easy who is  a complex network scientist and assa  raskin and britselvitel from her  speeches  who uh have worked before on uh other  non-human species communication and last  but not least  we also have the creative media and  communication side  with uh then dilani from uh  national geographic uh christian michael  lawrence  movie director and uh james nestor  journalist  and as david mentioned uh in june said  it was announced as  one of the audacious projects funded by  private donors under the aegis of  ted and it is really their generosity  that  will make our dream a reality so this is  something we're  truly indebted for for the trust and  support  so let me say a few words about how we  are going to collect  this data set and here we are primarily  counting on three classes of  assets uh or types of sensors that will  be  deployed in the watches of dominica and  uh first we'll be using static sensor  arrays to collect massive amounts of  background  by acoustic data and these are big  devices you can see  uh in this picture that was from uh  provided by rui  they are similar in their design to some  of the military underwater equipment  that is used to detect submarines on  large areas and distances and each such  array  is attached to a buoy that will consist  of multiple hydrophones  in rigid configuration that we call oil  recording units  that will be positioned at different  depths with about 200 to  300 meter increments and the use of  multiple hydrophones and multiple buoys  we  plan having four at least will allow to  detect the location of whales and track  them over time  and this will help to solve the  so-called cocktail party problem  the need to unmix multiple sources of  sound in recording which  arises when using just a few microphones  like it  was done in the past and typically done  marine uh by acoustic uh recordings  the boys will also record metadata such  as the ocean and weather conditions and  water temperature  that is unnecessary for for example for  calibrating the speed of sound  necessary for doing the translation  and uh despite these tracking  capabilities we are not sure yet if the  boys will provide enough accuracy to  uniquely identify the  whales and you can think of a possible  situation  when a whale disappears from the field  of view or maybe stop stopping for a  long while and then reappears  so we'll probably not be able to say for  sure if uh it is the same whale  re-appearing or a new one though we are  also considering using spectral  information  in order to try to do speaker  recognition and  probably what also helps is that usually  their  sound is directed so we can try to  estimate the  direction of arrival but there are still  many open questions here  the second type of assets we'll be using  for data collection there are tags  that are attached to whales and you can  see here an example of such tags  of the same type chain and his team were  using in the  dominican from whale project to do some  of the recordings  and the tags are attached to the whale  body on  the head and records not only the whale  by acoustics but also signals from  inertial and pressure sensors  allowing to reconstruct the motion the  diving pattern and  other behavioral patterns and maybe in  some of them you can also do  a video recording uh the main limitation  of current tags is battery life and  adhesion  so this is a big engineering challenge  to make sure that they can  be firmly and gently attached to the  whale for a long period that will be  enough to collect  interesting recordings and here i should  stress that  our overall philosophy is to act in a  minimally invasive way that doesn't harm  or disturb the  the animals so we plan using advanced  active suction mechanisms  for example to attach these tags and uh  there are again many challenges here as  well how to place  these tanks so far uh it's just a boat  with a very long stick  uh maybe we can do something that is a  little bit more sophisticated using  robotic systems the use of the tags  one of the things that will allow is to  uniquely identify the whales  in conversations and associate also the  metadata such as behavioral patterns  with background recordings that are  produced of the static  sensor arrays and by reconciling these  different sources of information  we'll be able uh to have a full picture  about both individual whales  and the population and finally as a  backup option and there's a way to  ensure that we don't have any blind  spots  in our data collection we also consider  developing swimming robots that could be  deployed in  some interesting locations where the  crowds of whales and collect  data complementing these static arrays  and attacks  and here again there is a wide spectrum  of solutions from  simple drifters such what is shown here  so  more sophisticated self-propelled robots  with automatic navigation  so this is still again something where  we are defining and overall  our goal is to uh to do a large scale  longitudinal study of sperm whale  populations over  several years and we are striving to  collect  uh massive amounts of bio acoustic data  suitable for  machine learning approaches and we  believe 